362:
Before operating a pawnshop or becoming an employee of a pawnshop, any person must first file an application with the municipal police department for a permit to operate or be employed in the pawnshop, pursuant to the following:
(1) The application shall be made on an annual basis;
(2) No permit shall be issued until a fee in an amount established by action of the city council, a copy of which is on file in the office of the city clerk, is paid to the city;
(3) The application shall state the physical address of the pawnshop;
(4) The application shall contain the full name, street address, mailing address, phone number, facsimile number, e-mail address, Social Security number, and date of birth of any employee, owner or pawnbroker;
(5) The chief of police or any other officer of the city designated by the city council, shall investigate each applicant for such license and shall report to the city council whether such applicant is a person of good character and has not been convicted of a criminal offense as indicated in this article.
(Ord. of 6-17-2013, § I)

398:
(a) Application for a permit shall be made upon a form provided by the city. The city manager shall have applications available on request. The applicant shall truthfully state and provide in full the information requested on the application, to wit:
(1) Name and address of present place of residence and length of residence at such address, also business address if other than present address;
(2) Address of place of residence during the past three years if other than present address;
(3) Age of applicant;
(4) Physical description of the applicant;
(5) Name and address of the person or association whom the applicant is employed by or represents, copies of credentials or documents verifying the relationship, and the length of time of such employment or representation;
(6) Name and address of employer during the past three years if other than the present employer;
(7) Description sufficient for identification of the subject matter of the soliciting in which the applicant will engage;
(8) Period of time for which the certificate is applied;
(9) Proposed route, including streets to be included on each day, which applicant intends to follow;
(10) The date, or approximate date, of the latest previous application for permit under this chapter, if any;
(11) Whether the applicant has ever been convicted of a felony, a crime of moral turpitude, or any other violation of any state or federal law, regulation, or ordinance;
(12) Whether the applicant or any business or entity represented by the applicant has ever been the subject of an investigation by any governmental agency for false advertising, deceptive trade practices, or unfair business practices;
(13) Whether the applicant or any business or entity represented by the applicant has ever had any similar solicitation permit suspended or revoked by any governmental agency for any reason;
(14) Names of magazines, books, or journals to be sold;
(15) Names of the three most recent communities where the applicant has solicited house to house;
(16) Proposed method of operation;
(17) Description and license plate number of vehicle intended to be operated by applicant;
(18) Signature of applicant.
(b) All statements made by the applicant upon the application or in connection therewith shall be under oath.
(c) The applicant shall submit to fingerprinting taken at a Georgia Bureau of Investigation designated Georgia Applicant Processing Service (GAPS) fingerprinting site. The fingerprints will be submitted to the Georgia Bureau of Investigation/GCIC where the subject's fingerprints will be compared against its criminal file and submitted to the Federal Bureau of Investigations for comparison of nationwide records. The results of the Federal Bureau of Investigation check will be returned to the Georgia Bureau of Investigation/GCIC, which will disseminate the state and national results to the City of Pooler Police Department.
(d) In compliance with Public Law 92-544, which provides for the rendering of a fitness determination, the City of Pooler Police Department will render a fitness determination based upon the results of the criminal background check. In rendering a fitness determination, the City of Pooler Police Department will decide whether the record subject has been convicted of (or is under pending indictment for) any felony or a misdemeanor which involved a crime or moral turpitude, forcer or threat of force, possession of weapons, controlled substances, or was a sex-related offense.
(e) The city manager, or city manager's designee, shall cause to be kept with the city an accurate record of every application received and acted upon together with all other information and data pertaining thereto and all permits issued under the provisions of this article, and of the denial of applications. Applications for permits shall be numbered in consecutive order as filed, and every permit issued shall be identified with the duplicate number of the application upon which it was issued. A record subject may request and receive a copy of his/her criminal history record information from the City of Pooler Police Department. Should the record subject seek to amend or correct his/her record, he/she must contact the Georgia Bureau of Investigation/GCIC for a Georgia state record or the Federal Bureau of Investigation for record from other jurisdictions maintained in its file.
(f) No permit shall be issued to any person who has been convicted of a felony or crime of moral turpitude, nor to any person who has been convicted of a violation of any of the provisions of this article, nor to any person whose permit issued hereunder has previously been revoked as herein provided.
(g) The fee for a permit shall be as determined by the city council.
(Ord. of 6-18-2018, § I; Ord. No. 2020-06.A, § I, 6-15-20)

